WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:03.860
Hello Internet!

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:09.170
Welcome to Film Theory, where no matter how
much the Theorist Dynasty grows, we promise

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:10.910
to never get a big head.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.944
Well, theorists, my buddy Paul Rudd is back

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.580
With a new hit movie, Ant-Man and the Wasp
Quantumania.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.260
Maybe “hit” is overselling it a bit based
on audience review scores for this one.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.480
So let me just try that again.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.080
Of all the MCU movies, Ant-Man Quantumania
is one of them.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.590
Says a lot about a movie when the most excitement
I get is the final title that reveals that

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:37.100
the words Ant-Man were actually hidden in
quANTuMANia the entire time.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.870
I was like, Whoa, no way.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:42.700
It’s like when you find out that the FedEx
logo has a secret arrow hiding inside of it.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.900
But with this latest release, we officially
have entered the beginning of MCU Phase 5,

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.230
where, as the marketing hype says, nothing
will ever be the same, which is actually very

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.949
much the same for Marvel movies.

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:56.770
Hey, guys, maybe you just consider changing
up your press releases every once in a while.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.672
Or you know what? Nevermind. Scratch that.

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:01.980
Maybe I should be careful what I wish for,
considering change is the thing that single

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.330
handedly drove this movie into the ground.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.310
Well, that and soggy bottom MODOK.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:10.240
Seriously, this is like the alternate universe
if they had waited to reveal Ugly Sonic is

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.320
part of the movie.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.700
Here we're just stuck with this thing for
2 hours.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.110
Anyway, if you're really into PG Rick and
Morty episodes, then this one is the movie for you.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.799
Whether you like the film or not, something
that I've seen everyone agree on is that things

00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:26.189
felt off about the story.

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:29.880
Scenes that felt awkwardly placed, moments
that resolved to quickly character arcs that

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.659
were more like character cliffs.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:35.777
And this time, there's no one around to break
the fourth wall and argue with producers about it.

00:01:39.748 --> 00:01:43.560
Something major changed here, and I'm not
just talking about normal production stuff

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.570
where things get left on the cutting room
floor.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.040
There's another movie hiding in here somewhere,
one that got chopped up, shuffled around,

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.780
re-edited, rearranged and reshot for some
reason.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.460
And I want to know what that original movie
was.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.020
I want to see the story that this was trying
to tell because my boy Ant-Man deserved better.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.759
I want to still care about this franchise
and its Pharoh Kang that looks like it just

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:05.049
rolled out of the party city.

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:07.390
I want to, but it’s getting real hard.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:12.140
And if I need to stop caring about this franchise,
I want to know what went wrong along the way.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.140
Short version.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:13.640
Get it? Short ver…

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:14.140
Anyway.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.700
I think at one point Scott Lang's adventure
to the quantum realm not only had a very different,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.870
much darker ending, it also had a more complicated,
morally complex story arc for the main character.

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:27.040
But here's the biggest twist I think solving
what this movie could have been tells us a

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.967
fascinating tale of the major mistakes that
Marvel and the rest of Hollywood are making right now.

00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:35.890
Okay, so let me give you the quick recap of the
movie we got so we can then, you know, actually

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.069
piece together the movie we should have got.

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:42.500
So it's 2025 and Scott Lang is kind of sort
of retired from Ant-Maning since there really

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.480
hasn't been an Avengers level threat lately.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.464
Except for a city being taken hostage, a giant
Kaiju battle over Egypt, the multiverse ripping

00:02:48.464 --> 00:02:51.529
apart, and the complete rampage of a deadly
witch gone rogue.

00:02:51.529 --> 00:02:54.090
Everyone just forgot Scott's number for those.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.910
So instead, he wrote a book available on Audible.com,
I have no doubt.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:01.739
Meanwhile, Hope is running Pym Industries
as a do-gooder Girlboss, Hank is collecting

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:06.790
fat paychecks for mildly reacting to CGI ants
and Janet is definitely not talking about

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:09.900
what she did while stuck in the quantum realm
for the past 30 years.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.499
Seriously, don't even bring it up, she just
doesn't have time to talk about it right now.

00:03:12.499 --> 00:03:13.183
Okay, guy?

00:03:13.183 --> 00:03:17.359
As for Scott's daughter, Cassie, she's all
grown up into a completely different human being.

00:03:17.359 --> 00:03:20.930
She's also grown up to be an activist stealing
cop cars and protesting for the land rights

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:21.930
of blip victims.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.950
So basically, she's a flag smasher.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:26.920
But don't worry, it's okay for her to do it
because she's the hero's daughter.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.069
She also thinks that the grown ups in her
life are kind of resting on their saved-the-world-that-one-time

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:34.040
laurels, Instead of being as angry about all
this stuff like she is.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.240
Then everyone gets zapped into the quantum
realm so this whole boomers versus zoomers

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.704
allegory can play out with cool monsters and
lasers.

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:44.210
The good guys get split up between Hank, Hope
and Janet.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:48.360
So she can very slowly reveal the plot, while
Scott and Cassie get caught up with a group

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.989
of refugees being oppressed by an evil dictator,
Kang the Conqueror.

00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:53.730
And yeah, it's going exactly where you think
it is.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:57.049
This is the kind of cause that Cassie is super
passionate about, and she wants to use her

00:03:57.049 --> 00:03:58.120
powers to help.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.459
Scott, meanwhile, says it's not their fight
and he's only focused on getting her home.

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:05.069
And with that, we've set up yet again the
clear ideological divide between these two

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:08.700
characters that we’ll then see inevitably
play out over the next 2 hours of

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:09.700
*Record Scratch*

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.550
Oh, wait, Scott just decides to agree with
Cassie.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.730
Okay, that was quick to resolve itself.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.200
But anyway, Cassie gets captured by Kang,
forcing Ant-Man to cooperate.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.690
They escape and ultimately return, storming
the castle with their ragtag army of refugees.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:26.660
Kang blasts a lot of people with the disintegration
laser, but then makes the strategic decision

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.000
to only punch all the named characters.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.510
Eventually, the script writers remember it's
an Ant-Man movie.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:34.770
So Kang is ultimately overwhelmed by an army
of CGI ants.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.280
Oh, and MODOK is here.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.970
And then right before they escape the quantum
realm through a portal, Kang returns.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:43.380
Scott makes the ultimate self-sacrifice by
pushing the rest of his family to safety while

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.108
he gets the beat down of his life.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:49.390
just as it looks like he's about to die.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:52.254
Hope comes in through the closing portal to
finish off Kang for good.

00:04:52.254 --> 00:04:52.773
Maybe.

00:04:52.773 --> 00:04:56.550
Scott and Hope get a big, dramatic, heroes-looking-out-over-victory
wide-shot.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.479
Perfect for an ambiguous but hopeful cut to
black cliffhanger.

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:03.100
And thus the movie ends with Scott and Hope
trapped in the quantum realm with Cassie and

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.575
the rest of the young Avengers having to work
to get them ba...

00:05:05.575 --> 00:05:06.184
*Record Scratch*

00:05:06.184 --> 00:05:07.770
Wait, another record scratch?

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.539
That is like two times we've used the same
bit this episode.

00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:14.440
No, actually, instead of being stranded in
the quantum realm, Cassie immediately opens

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.100
another portal to the real world…

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.100
Somehow.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.040
And we get a weirdly abrupt, happy ending
coda almost entirely in VO, where Scott wonders

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.280
whether Kang will return.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:25.169
And then two post-credits scenes in the end
of the film teaser confirmed that yes.

00:05:25.169 --> 00:05:26.500
Yes he will return.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:27.520
So that's the movie.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.590
And largely it works as a film, I guess.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.080
Are there plot threads left awkwardly dangling?

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.080
Absolutely.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:34.859
Is it an Ant-Man movie?

00:05:34.859 --> 00:05:36.058
No, definitely not.

00:05:36.058 --> 00:05:39.639
But all of those things weren't what set my
theorist senses a-tingling.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:42.830
What really convinced me to start digging
deeper here was the ending.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:46.720
Everything that happens from when Cassie turns
the portal back on looks a lot like something

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.509
that was put together after the fact in post-production,
using a bunch of recycled B-roll, a few minimal

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:52.840
reshoots and new voiceover.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.030
I mean, Scott's self-sacrifice fake-out lasts
less than a minute.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:00.720
Just from a visual storytelling perspective
alone, doesn't fit the very affectionate hug

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.690
it out family above all tone that the rest
of the movie sets up.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.073
No big “How am I going to get out this time?” moment.

00:06:06.073 --> 00:06:08.390
No big reunion scene on the other side of
the portal.

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:11.050
No restatement of the lessons that were learned
along the way.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:12.780
This is Marvel Studios here, people.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.970
If that stuff ain't in there it’s because
it wasn't shot to begin with.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:17.419
Kevin runs himself a tight ship.

00:06:21.711 --> 00:06:26.410
And that conclusion is supported by leaks
that happened as far back as last September,

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:29.570
which said that while no one was meant to
die in the end, Hope and Scott were meant

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.310
to get stranded in the quantum realm.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:32.740
That shot of Cassie turning on the portal.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.330
I suspect that it was originally going to
be her activating something else entirely,

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.199
possibly even as part of a post-credits scene.

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:42.060
My personal head canon is that she was flipping
a switch to signal her father's old team to

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:43.060
come help them.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:45.410
Our first confirmation that The Avengers are
coming back.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:47.130
Except this time they're young.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:50.090
You wouldn't even need to know who the Avengers
actually were at this point.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.750
Just a tense zoom on a digital screen and
then: ASSEMBLE.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.310
But also a self-sacrificial ending here makes
logical sense as well.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:58.707
See while it might be a bit of an exaggeration
to say

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:05.940
One thing you probably did notice is that
heroic self-sacrifice happens a lot.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.950
Far and away, the MCU’s third favorite trope
after bad dads and villains who are actually

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:11.380
evil versions of the hero.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:15.379
Thor giving up Jane to stop Loki, Wanda lose
and vision to destroy the Mind Stone, Black

00:07:15.379 --> 00:07:19.190
Widow yeeting herself off the cliff for the
Soul Stone, Wenwu protecting his son from

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:20.340
The Dweller In Darkness.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.190
Peter Parker giving up his life to fix the
multiverse and of course, Iron Man's sacrifice

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.400
to snap away Thanos.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.349
Hack, the way that we know that Captain America
is the goodest of the good guys is that he's

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:32.919
a jump on the grenade guy at the beginning
of his story without needing to learn any

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:35.039
lessons about humility or respect on the way.

00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:39.580
Be ready to lay down your life for others
is the moral baseline of the MCU.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.199
It's the thing that makes the good guys, “the
good guys” here.

00:07:42.199 --> 00:07:45.099
Even newer characters are being put to the
same tried and true test.

00:07:45.099 --> 00:07:49.370
In Multiverse of Madness, America Chavez is
only able to defeat Evil Wanda after she says

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:52.220
that she'd be willing to sacrifice herself
for the good of the many.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:56.190
Then and only then is she able to become a
marvel hero who can sucker punch someone who

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:57.889
once held her own against Thanos.

00:07:57.889 --> 00:08:01.780
What all of these stories have in common is
that the sacrifice is the payoff to an arc

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.039
in the growth of the character.

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:05.599
Thor learns how to put others' needs ahead
of his own.

00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:07.040
America stops running from her problem.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.720
Tony has been trying to make up for being
an arms dealer since he became Iron Man.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.510
And here's the thing, Ant-Man movies have
already used the trope.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:18.260
In the first film Scott shrinks to quantum
size to protect Cassie 1.0 and therefore stop

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:22.142
the man who would be MODOK, even though as
far as he knew there was no coming back from that choice.

00:08:22.142 --> 00:08:25.530
Why then would they do the same kind of payoff
here again in the third outing?

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:26.870
Well, here's one possibility.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:30.220
What if that subplot about Scott and Cassie
butting heads over when and how to get involved

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:33.940
in other people's fights that just kind of
resolves itself in one scene, was originally

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.789
meant to be a much bigger deal?

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:38.070
As in, the entire point of the movie kind
of bigger.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:41.479
One thing I made fun of in my plot summary
about the movie, but that's really frustrating

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:45.279
when you actually watch it, is how slowly
Janet reveals information about Kang.

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:47.540
She knows that he's been down here the entire
time.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:50.230
In fact, she's the one who helped Kang rise
to power.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:53.790
She's the reason all of this is happening
and why all these Rick and Morty rejects are

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:54.790
being oppressed.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:57.410
But the movie delivers that information to
us in a weird way.

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:01.690
They drip feed it to us at a snail's pace,
like much longer than a typical movie would

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:02.690
deliver this information.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:06.470
They keep the name of the villain secret for
a bunch of the runtime of the film, referring

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:07.709
to Kang as “Him”.

00:09:07.709 --> 00:09:10.529
All of them talking in hushed tones much longer
than you would expect.

00:09:10.529 --> 00:09:14.329
And the fact that Janet is split up from Scott
and Cassie means that even when she does finally

00:09:14.329 --> 00:09:17.320
find the time to explain her backstory like
an hour into the film.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:19.970
Scott and Cassie still wouldn't know the danger
that's around them.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:24.040
I suspect that the film was originally structured
this way so that Kang's appearance and the

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:27.680
fact that he's evil would be a big middle
of the movie reveal.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.720
Now, that might seem like a big leap, but
consider what this does to the character arcs

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.720
in the film.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:35.300
What if, in an earlier version of the movie,
Scott didn't take Cassie's side right away?

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:37.670
Instead, he kept trying to only protect her.

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:42.021
As a result, he takes up Kang's go-get-a-thing-for-me
offer willingly, despite her objections, because

00:09:42.021 --> 00:09:45.400
from his perspective; Hey, we don't know what's
happening down here really.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:46.400
He seems to be the guy in charge.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.170
Why are we assuming the weird aliens who made
us drink goo are the good guys?

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:53.810
That then reinforces the opening beats of
the movie, the generational gap and their

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:54.810
different priorities.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.950
Then it all goes wrong and the rest of the
story plays out mostly the same.

00:09:57.950 --> 00:09:58.950
“Hey, I'm Kang.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.370
Thanks for helping me achieve my true power.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:03.020
Now, if you'll excuse me, I'm just going to
go oppress everything.”

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.020
Look at what that does.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.120
Scott suddenly realizes that he was wrong
all along.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:10.920
He feels guilty for enabling this villain
and needs to do an MCU heroic sacrifice (trademark)

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.230
to atone for it, thereby reaffirming his good
guy status while also establishing Cassie

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:18.430
as having had the clearer, more righteous
moral judgment all along.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:21.720
There's even this really strange moment in
the actual film that makes me feel like we're

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.130
really on to something here.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:26.920
Just before the big final battle, Scott stands
up in giant man mode to confront Kang.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.942
And he has this angry line, shouting more
seriously than we've ever heard this character sound.

00:10:30.942 --> 00:10:31.852
“We had a deal.

00:10:31.852 --> 00:10:35.819
Our word is our bond!” which, okay, he's
mad.

00:10:35.819 --> 00:10:36.639
He should be.

00:10:36.639 --> 00:10:41.209
But why is he outraged that Kang, a guy he
just found out existed, a guy that he only

00:10:41.209 --> 00:10:45.728
knows as an evil dictator, is Scott really
that surprised that he was dishonest with him?

00:10:45.728 --> 00:10:49.420
He has no reason to have assumed that he could
trust Kang, unless all of that is a remnant

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:51.269
from a version of the story where he did.

00:10:51.269 --> 00:10:53.930
I got to tell you theorists, all the pieces
are in place for us.

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:55.310
It fixes the broken story arcs.

00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:57.389
It explains the awkwardly paced storytelling.

00:10:57.389 --> 00:11:00.070
It corrects the uncomfortable and reshot ending.

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:04.910
Kang's heel turn was meant to be a twist reveal
rather than a core part of all the marketing.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:09.740
So why then did they make such a drastic change
and in the process butcher this movie to pieces?

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:12.360
Because I suspect that this wasn't meant to
be a Phase 5 movie.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:14.714
This was meant to be the end of phase 4.

00:11:14.714 --> 00:11:15.549
Let me explain.

00:11:15.549 --> 00:11:19.000
If I'm right about what this movie's plot
and ending were meant to be, look at what

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:23.350
we're left with: a dark story about oppression,
not trusting authority, and an ideological

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:26.150
divide between older and younger generations
of heroes.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:30.700
That doesn't sound like an Ant-Man movie,
but it does sound like a marvel Phase 4 project.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:35.950
For all its disjointed weirdness, Phase 4
did have itself a consistent theme: Instability.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.149
Characters grappling with the realization
that defeating Thanos, literally thwarting

00:11:39.149 --> 00:11:42.380
the end of the universe, didn't end every
other problem forever.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:44.360
There's still suffering, injustice, inequality.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:45.970
Some of it got even worse.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:49.660
And now there are big disagreements over what
to do about that, especially between people

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:53.269
who feel like the biggest fight ever has already
been won, and those who think the fight has

00:11:53.269 --> 00:11:56.560
only just started, which of course falls across
generational lines.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.089
And it's here that we pit a rising crop of
young heroes who want to fight the power and

00:12:00.089 --> 00:12:05.620
often hail from backgrounds that are, well,
less significantly heavy on white guys named Chris.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:07.130
Against older heroes who just want to retire.

00:12:07.130 --> 00:12:09.320
Hawkeye and Ant-Man just want to be dads.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:10.779
The Hulk is into meditation now.

00:12:10.779 --> 00:12:13.519
The entire nation of Wakanda just wants to
be left alone again.

00:12:13.519 --> 00:12:17.350
And if you're really into heavy handed metaphors
in a Multiverse Of Madness, Scarlet Witch

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:22.060
goes full super villain by trying to rip the
lifeforce out of a Latina teenager in order

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:27.250
to resurrect a fake version of the American
dream that only ever existed in her imagination.

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:31.279
Even the blackest sheep of Phase 4, Thor:
Love and Thunder, got in on these same themes

00:12:31.279 --> 00:12:33.250
of instability and generational change.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:36.269
I mean, the bad guy wants to literally kill
every God.

00:12:36.269 --> 00:12:38.640
You don't get much more anti-authority than
that.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.720
And the movie ultimately agrees with Gorr.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.170
We see that the gods do suck.

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:46.480
Thor is only able to win by blowing up his
franchise's chosen-one conceit, by declaring

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:50.570
that all the kids are worthy to be Thor and
that anything from random junk to a teddy

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:52.519
bear can be as good as Mjolnir.

00:12:52.519 --> 00:12:55.459
That right there, that is Phase 4's narrative
in one place.

00:12:55.459 --> 00:12:56.610
The system is falling apart.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:59.550
The people in charge are either corrupt or
failing to fix it.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:02.670
The young people know what's what and they're
the ones who are going to save us.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:04.430
The righteous path for any O.G.

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:08.829
Avenger still kicking around is to aid, mentor
and pass that torch to the next generation.

00:13:08.829 --> 00:13:11.830
Ant-Man getting tricked into empowering a
time hopping Multiversal dictator?

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:15.459
Him and the Wasp getting trapped and needing
help from a younger generation of heroes?

00:13:15.459 --> 00:13:19.150
Sounds like a solid way to bring all these
themes together and end this arc of storytelling

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:23.079
on a cliffhanger that then gets us eager for
the next major chapter of the story in the

00:13:23.079 --> 00:13:24.720
new Phase 5.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.174
So then why would they have changed Quantumania’s
Story?

00:13:28.467 --> 00:13:32.300
But in this case, I'm talking about the real
world blip: 2020 Covid happened, not only

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.930
delaying movie release dates, but the entire
real world global economy.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.690
Movie production was at a standstill.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:42.829
Theaters were closing, people were turning
to *GASP* YouTube and TikTok for their entertainment.

00:13:42.829 --> 00:13:47.339
Suddenly, the pressure was on for Marvel to
single handedly save the cinema, things had

00:13:47.339 --> 00:13:50.840
to be churned out as fast as possible whenever
they were able to get finished.

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:54.080
Looking at Marvel's original release schedule
for Phase 4, you see that the plan was to

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:58.130
gradually introduce the multiverse as opposed
to throwing it all out there at once.

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:01.149
No longer was there a chance to slowly build
up to the reveal of Kang.

00:14:01.149 --> 00:14:03.110
He had to be put out there front and center.

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:04.470
People loved Thanos, after all.

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:05.940
They'll have to love this guy too.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:10.140
Make Endgame happen again because the world
almost ended and the movie biz is on the verge

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:11.140
of dying.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:14.740
Pander to how corporate suits see Gen Z. Make
sure the fans are satisfied by giving them

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:16.490
MODOK, they've been asking for him for a while.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:20.149
Bigger scale, bolder choices, multiscreen
viewing experiences.

00:14:20.149 --> 00:14:23.199
Look at this graph of MCU content released
by year.

00:14:23.199 --> 00:14:26.921
2021 had five times the amount of content
of any other year before it.

00:14:26.921 --> 00:14:30.610
It was a rush, a move of desperation to recover
after a year without theaters.

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:34.308
Except, in all of this rush they forgot why
people came out to these movies in the first place.

00:14:34.308 --> 00:14:38.500
The stories, the characters, not years of
a plot working together or fan service for

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:39.589
the sake of fan service.

00:14:39.589 --> 00:14:41.620
Earned moments with tight narratives.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:44.870
Compelling stories that made sense without
massive plot holes.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:48.641
Not an overreliance on quippy dialog, but
dialog that was funny because yeah, that would

00:14:48.641 --> 00:14:50.910
be what that character would say in that moment.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:55.350
What's killing the MCU right now, and especially
QuantumANTia here, are moves that are made

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:59.519
out of panic, self-doubt, a lack of confidence
in the stories that they plan to tell, reedits

00:14:59.519 --> 00:15:03.959
that result in choppy, uneven, compromised
narratives as films are hacked to pieces.

00:15:03.959 --> 00:15:07.339
It took the Marvel brand, which used to act
as a seal of quality, and it's now reduced

00:15:07.339 --> 00:15:10.769
it into something that, yeah, maybe I'll go
see that when it airs on streaming.

00:15:10.769 --> 00:15:15.000
The last time a studio acted in this sort
of panicked way, rushing to get to the next big event?

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.390
That was DC, and we're still watching how
long it's taking them to turn things around.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:19.790
And there you have it, theorists.

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:22.870
The darker story for Ant-Man &amp; The Wasp Quantumania.

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:23.998
Are we right? Totally off?

00:15:23.998 --> 00:15:24.998
Will we ever know?

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:25.774
It's hard to say.

00:15:25.774 --> 00:15:28.889
But what I'm sure about is that this isn't
going to be the last time that this genre

00:15:28.889 --> 00:15:30.139
of theory comes up.

00:15:30.139 --> 00:15:33.790
The whole media industry is still putting
itself back together after years of huge world

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:34.790
shaping events.

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:38.510
And even if it didn't play out as dark as
it might have, the most relatable story that

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:43.389
this movie tried to tell is that you can't
really unchange the world, only decide how

00:15:43.389 --> 00:15:45.100
you are going to respond to it.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:49.179
But hey, that's just a theory, A FILM THEORY!

00:15:49.179 --> 00:15:50.549
aaaaaaaand cut.

